WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.210
Let's say 2022 is going to
be the year when I start to

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.940
discover the languages of India.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.150
Hi There, Steve Kaufmann here and
today, I'm going to talk about new

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.340
year's resolutions, new year's goals.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.130
Remember if you enjoy my videos
where I talk about language

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.660
learning, please subscribe.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:19.590
If you listen to me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.340
So 2022, it's a new year.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.530
Uh, typically a new year is a time for
us to assess what we have been doing.

00:00:29.264 --> 00:00:31.604
What we might do differently
in the coming year.

00:00:32.415 --> 00:00:36.915
Um, so I'm going to divide the,
the sort of language or my goals

00:00:36.915 --> 00:00:38.625
or my resolutions in two videos.

00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:41.745
One that I'll do today and then the
next one that I'll do in a few days.

00:00:42.254 --> 00:00:46.245
Today, I'm going to talk about
language goals, which languages, which

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:47.805
languages am I going to focus on?

00:00:47.805 --> 00:00:48.885
What are my plans?

00:00:49.394 --> 00:00:53.655
Um, and then the next time I'll talk
about language learning activities and

00:00:53.655 --> 00:00:58.205
how I plan to change some of my language
learning activities in the new year.

00:00:59.145 --> 00:01:04.489
First of all, I am by nature
an optimist, and I think 2022

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:05.810
is going to be a good year.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:10.250
Uh, I think in many countries of
the world, we've learned to live

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.920
under very difficult circumstances.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.210
We've learned the extent to
which we have to help each other.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:22.610
Uh, we need solidarity within
countries, within our cities, within

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:24.650
wherever we live and internationally.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:25.870
We have to work together.

00:01:26.145 --> 00:01:29.055
And I think there will be a
renewed feeling that all the

00:01:29.105 --> 00:01:34.395
minor things that cause us grief
and conflict are, are minor.

00:01:34.515 --> 00:01:37.725
And that the most important thing
thing for us to think about is

00:01:37.875 --> 00:01:39.855
how can we all cooperate better?

00:01:40.365 --> 00:01:43.665
Uh, and I think people are going to
realize that, and I think there's going to

00:01:43.665 --> 00:01:48.225
be this great sense of joy as we go back
to living our lives normally, because I

00:01:48.225 --> 00:01:50.205
think there will be a number of changes.

00:01:50.205 --> 00:01:52.845
I think there'll be more
medicines available to reduce

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:53.965
the burden on the hospitals.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:55.470
If we all...

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.430
end up getting a cold, but we don't
end up, you know, in the intensive

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.630
care unit of hospitals, then, you
know, this is just a condition that

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.710
we're going to be able to live with
and lead our lives normally, hopefully

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:12.210
with a renewed sense of how important
it is that we all support each other.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:13.650
That's my hope at least.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.250
And I'm an optimist now in so
far as languages are concerned.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.730
You may know that I have been
concentrating on Arabic and Persian.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.850
I originally sought out to learn
three middle Eastern languages:

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.310
Turkish, Persian, and Arabic.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:33.180
Uh, I then discovered how
difficult it is to get used to

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.400
the sort of Arabic writing system.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.690
So I said, I'll just focus on the two
languages that use the Arabic writing

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:44.415
system, namely Persian and Arabic, and
I'll put Turkish on the back burner.

00:02:44.774 --> 00:02:48.704
Turkish is a different language
system from, I mean, Farsi

00:02:48.704 --> 00:02:49.935
is an Indo-European language.

00:02:50.204 --> 00:02:51.915
Arabic is a Semitic language.

00:02:52.155 --> 00:02:55.005
Turkish is again a different
language system, but it is

00:02:55.005 --> 00:02:56.565
written in the Latin alphabet.

00:02:56.565 --> 00:03:01.185
And that makes it a lot easier,
especially for someone like me who likes

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:06.165
reading and listening as the main sort
of activity for learning languages.

00:03:07.095 --> 00:03:09.255
So it's been Arabic and Persian.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.690
Along the way I come up, you know, I came
up against the problem with Arabic that

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:21.120
there is standard Arabic, there's Egyptian
Arabic, there's Levantine Arabic, not to

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.280
mention, you know, the Gulf Arabic and,
and the, the sort of North African Arabic.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.480
And so I ended up sort of saying,
you know, I'm going to focus on

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.490
standard Arabic, and I'm going to
continue doing that because so much

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.940
of political discussion and history
books, and whatever's written

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.920
down, uh, people on television and
so forth speak standard Arabic.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.750
So I want to be able to access that.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.640
So I need my standard Arabic, but
I have put more and more effort

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.190
into certainly Egyptian Arabic.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.000
Uh, I've had a couple
of tutors from Egypt.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.329
Uh, we have more and more
content in Egyptian Arabic, and

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:01.950
I've been listening to these.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.080
We have the mini stories
in Egyptian Arabic.

00:04:04.815 --> 00:04:08.984
Uh, and of course, part of the motivation
is that there's so many movies in Egypt

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:13.755
that if I want to understand them, I
have to learn Egyptian Arabic, but I've

00:04:13.755 --> 00:04:19.904
also increased recently my attention
to  Arabic again, because first of

00:04:19.904 --> 00:04:23.925
all, there are movies from Lebanon,
maybe predominantly from Lebanon.

00:04:24.435 --> 00:04:30.275
I found a wonderful, uh, television
station in Jordan called Donya Ya Donya

00:04:30.275 --> 00:04:36.674
, which puts out lots of videos on YouTube
about everything from, you know, women

00:04:36.674 --> 00:04:41.174
losing weight to health issues to you
name it, all great stuff for learning.

00:04:41.474 --> 00:04:46.664
And as I get into the different forms of
Arabic, I'm more and more convinced that

00:04:46.664 --> 00:04:49.065
Arabic is just one, it's one language.

00:04:49.565 --> 00:04:54.614
I'm very glad that I started with
standard Arabic, and that gives me

00:04:54.614 --> 00:04:59.385
a base from which I can, you know,
branch into, uh, other forms of Arabic.

00:04:59.414 --> 00:05:01.065
And the goal is to understand.

00:05:01.515 --> 00:05:06.224
So I don't have a tremendous amount
of opportunity to use the language.

00:05:06.705 --> 00:05:12.164
Uh, and you know, if I were in Egypt
or if I were in Lebanon or, or,

00:05:12.195 --> 00:05:16.724
uh, you know, Jordan, then I would
probably try to speak more the way they

00:05:16.724 --> 00:05:18.885
do, but in a way it doesn't matter.

00:05:18.914 --> 00:05:20.414
There's a fair amount of flexibility.

00:05:20.835 --> 00:05:25.065
Uh, and you can mix in, you know,
standard Arabic, Egyptian Arabic, but

00:05:25.515 --> 00:05:29.414
from what I can gather, the Levantine
Arabic, somehow the Arab speakers

00:05:29.414 --> 00:05:31.015
all managed to understand each other.

00:05:31.495 --> 00:05:35.085
Uh, and they use some kind of a
mixture of different forms of Arabic

00:05:35.085 --> 00:05:36.705
and they, and they, they communicate.

00:05:36.885 --> 00:05:40.515
So I feel I can do, I can learn to
do the same, but I want to be able to

00:05:40.515 --> 00:05:42.555
understand the different forms of Arabic.

00:05:42.555 --> 00:05:46.815
So, first of all, with regards to
Arabic, I will continue my activities.

00:05:47.295 --> 00:05:52.215
Uh, I'm going to increasingly use,
you know, short segments of TV

00:05:52.215 --> 00:05:54.135
programs that I can find in YouTube.

00:05:55.485 --> 00:05:59.235
Uh, w what I tend to do is, uh,
try to get ahold of the MP3s.

00:06:00.015 --> 00:06:03.775
Uh, in order to transcribe it so
that I can bring that into LingQ

00:06:03.795 --> 00:06:05.385
and pick up the words and phrases.

00:06:05.775 --> 00:06:09.225
The automatic transcription
sometimes converts the sort of

00:06:09.225 --> 00:06:12.735
Levantine or Egyptian words into
standard Arabic, but that's okay.

00:06:12.735 --> 00:06:13.755
I hear what they're saying.

00:06:13.755 --> 00:06:16.935
And then I see the equivalent in
standard Arabic, but in some cases

00:06:16.935 --> 00:06:21.825
it actually does transcribe the sort
of regional word, uh, you know, that

00:06:21.885 --> 00:06:23.925
that is in the actual audio itself.

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:25.725
So that's working fine
and I'm enjoying it.

00:06:25.995 --> 00:06:28.745
So there's where I am with my Arabic.

00:06:29.205 --> 00:06:32.039
Uh, I may start speaking a little
more often, but I don't have that

00:06:32.039 --> 00:06:33.479
much time to speak to my tutor.

00:06:34.049 --> 00:06:35.729
Um, so that's, uh, Arabic.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.219
Persian: I'll just continue.

00:06:38.219 --> 00:06:39.270
I'm enjoying what I'm doing.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.359
I'm learning about Persian food,
Persian culture, Persian history.

00:06:42.359 --> 00:06:43.740
And I would just continue doing that.

00:06:44.609 --> 00:06:48.570
But I think the big thing that I
wanted to announce is that I am going

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:54.650
to start into some other languages
and the reason is, now that LingQ

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:58.170
5.0 is it's  available on iOS.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.500
Uh, it will soon be available
on the web version, the web

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.610
browser version and Android.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:09.360
And we did promise that once we
have LingQ 5.0 done, that we will be

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:15.370
adding languages for which we have
60 mini stories done by volunteers.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:17.520
Now, it's not gonna happen right away.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:23.940
Because obviously, although we've had QA
people testing LingQ 5.0, once we open

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.280
it up to everybody, we will find bugs.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:27.780
Our users will find bugs.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:30.930
And so we'll will be spending
some time before we get the...

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.260
LingQ 5.0 to sort of settle down.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.570
But once we do, we'll be
adding these languages.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:40.830
So first of all, I'll tell you which
languages are available to upload.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.350
Once we get around to doing it,
we won't do them all at once.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:47.265
And then I'll tell you which
ones I'm going to try and why.

00:07:47.354 --> 00:07:47.835
All right.

00:07:47.864 --> 00:07:52.515
So first of all, these are the languages
for which 60 mini stories have been done.

00:07:53.185 --> 00:07:55.505
Khmer, Which I may  not...

00:07:55.525 --> 00:07:55.905
We'll see.

00:07:56.354 --> 00:08:03.905
Punjabi, Welsh, Armenian, Icelandic,
Jordan, Lithuanian,  Georgian

00:08:03.905 --> 00:08:06.234
rather, Lithuanian, and Tagalog.

00:08:06.874 --> 00:08:08.895
All of those  have 60 mini stories done.

00:08:09.885 --> 00:08:12.854
Now for Luxembourgish, we have 45 stories.

00:08:13.544 --> 00:08:15.344
For Vietnamese, we have 25.

00:08:15.344 --> 00:08:16.784
For Swahili we have 20.

00:08:16.784 --> 00:08:18.094
For Thai we have 20.

00:08:18.515 --> 00:08:21.465
For Urdu we have 20 and
for Latvian we have 10.

00:08:22.005 --> 00:08:26.354
So obviously until those other
languages, until we get up to

00:08:26.354 --> 00:08:30.354
60 mini stories, we won't be
contemplating adding them to LingQ.

00:08:31.094 --> 00:08:35.414
Uh, if we are able to find volunteers
for these other languages to bring

00:08:35.414 --> 00:08:37.034
it up to 60, then we will add them.

00:08:37.365 --> 00:08:40.275
And if we have volunteers for
other languages and we can bring

00:08:40.275 --> 00:08:42.015
it up to 60, we will also add.

00:08:42.794 --> 00:08:46.965
Now, of the list where we have
60 many stories done here are

00:08:46.965 --> 00:08:48.315
the ones that I'm interested in.

00:08:48.915 --> 00:08:55.035
I'm interested in Punjabi because,
uh, just as it's so enjoyable here in

00:08:55.035 --> 00:09:02.115
Vancouver, uh, checking out of a grocery
store or whatever store to speak Persian.

00:09:02.145 --> 00:09:04.545
And my, my doctor is from Iran.

00:09:04.725 --> 00:09:07.755
My barber is from Iran,
Iranians everywhere.

00:09:07.965 --> 00:09:10.885
So I have a lot of
opportunity to use Persian.

00:09:11.465 --> 00:09:16.605
Well, we also have a lot of people
from the Punjab, uh, primarily

00:09:16.605 --> 00:09:18.255
Sikhs living here in Vancouver.

00:09:18.705 --> 00:09:22.425
And I think it would be a lot of fun
to surprise some of those people,

00:09:22.635 --> 00:09:24.405
by speaking to them in Punjabi.

00:09:24.855 --> 00:09:28.635
Now I may not go into great
depth here, like with Persian

00:09:28.635 --> 00:09:32.625
and Arabic, I'm so committed to
getting sort of to a B2 level there.

00:09:32.935 --> 00:09:34.755
I don't want to abandon those.

00:09:34.965 --> 00:09:39.435
So I think if I do the mini stories
in Punjabi and a few other things,

00:09:39.435 --> 00:09:44.025
I'll get to the point where I have,
perhaps have a short conversation with

00:09:44.025 --> 00:09:47.745
someone, uh, give them the impression
that I speak the language when I don't.

00:09:48.235 --> 00:09:51.195
And that gives me a certain amount
of, uh, sense of achievement.

00:09:51.495 --> 00:09:52.815
So that would be for Punjabi.

00:09:53.085 --> 00:09:56.325
So Khmer, Welsh I don't
think I'm going to tackle.

00:09:56.595 --> 00:09:59.865
Uh, Armenian, Icelandic, Georgian.

00:09:59.955 --> 00:10:02.145
Uh, I don't see an
opportunity to use them here.

00:10:02.565 --> 00:10:05.505
On the other hand with Georgian,
you know, my wife and I, you know,

00:10:05.505 --> 00:10:11.115
I bought this book, uh, Iranian
cooking book, but it had Kurdish food,

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:14.220
Georgian food, all kinds of different
food from that part of the world.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:18.300
And it's not impossible in
the future that we might go to

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.290
Turkey and Georgia and Iran.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.640
And if that were the case, then I
would at least do the mini stories

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:29.680
in Georgia, but that's not on my plan
for, in my plan for the year 2022.

00:10:30.955 --> 00:10:32.635
Uh, Lithuanian as well.

00:10:32.635 --> 00:10:34.255
I don't see myself doing that.

00:10:34.405 --> 00:10:37.735
I might poke around, but I
won't commit much time to it.

00:10:38.235 --> 00:10:41.455
Tagalog on the other hand is of interest
because we have a lot of people from

00:10:41.455 --> 00:10:46.105
the Philippines here and you run into
them at the, you know, for example,

00:10:46.385 --> 00:10:49.615
the lab where I do my blood test or
you see a lot of people in different

00:10:49.615 --> 00:10:51.295
professions from the Philippines.

00:10:51.865 --> 00:10:57.835
Uh, in fact, we had some work done in our
kitchen and our, our contractor was, uh,

00:10:57.855 --> 00:10:59.695
they were a couple from the Philippines.

00:11:00.015 --> 00:11:03.285
And a lot of the, I wouldn't say a lot,
but some of the trades that they brought

00:11:03.285 --> 00:11:04.635
in were also from the Philippines.

00:11:04.875 --> 00:11:07.315
So there's an opportunity to use Tagalog.

00:11:07.585 --> 00:11:11.595
So Tagalog and Punjabi would be
two languages where I'm quite

00:11:11.595 --> 00:11:14.805
motivated to do the mini stories
so that I can get to some kind of

00:11:14.805 --> 00:11:17.985
a basic conversational level maybe.

00:11:18.525 --> 00:11:20.745
Uh, but the main focus...

00:11:20.805 --> 00:11:26.145
oh, and one other thing, if we ever get or
do, if we get the 20 story, mini stories

00:11:26.145 --> 00:11:28.185
up to 60, then I would also do Urdu.

00:11:28.675 --> 00:11:33.390
And we already have Gujarati uh, and we
also have a lot of Israelis in Canada,

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:38.699
so I might just, once I do the Punjabi,
which would be the initial motivation,

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:42.839
then I might just poke around the
Gujarati because I do come across,

00:11:42.839 --> 00:11:45.689
I know I have some friends who are
Ismaili and just to throw some things

00:11:45.689 --> 00:11:50.069
at them in Gujarati  , I don't know
what level of difficulty is involved.

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:51.959
Uh, I don't know how they're different.

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:54.220
I don't know how different
the writing systems are.

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:58.455
Uh, but let's say 2022 is going
to be the year when I start to

00:11:58.455 --> 00:12:01.305
discover the languages of India.

00:12:02.235 --> 00:12:06.765
And if we end up eventually with Hindi,
I may also do something in Hindi,

00:12:06.915 --> 00:12:11.415
just as in Arabic, I'm exploring,
you know, Levantine Arabic, Egyptian,

00:12:11.415 --> 00:12:13.095
Arabic, as well as standard Arabic.

00:12:13.545 --> 00:12:19.425
Then who knows where my exploration of
the languages of the Indian sub-continent,

00:12:19.845 --> 00:12:25.200
uh, will take me, but that's going
to be a major, uh, focus of 2022,

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:29.820
uh, along with, uh, just dabbling in,
in Tagalog so that, uh, you know, I

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:33.780
can surprise some of the, uh, recent
immigrants from the Philippines that

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:35.730
I might come across here in Vancouver.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:42.840
So those are my sort of language goals,
sort of stick with Arabic, get to a better

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:47.550
level of comprehension in, in, uh, uh,
standard Arabic, Egyptian Arabic, and

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:53.084
Levantine Arabic continue with my Persian
and one thing about goals is I don't want

00:12:53.084 --> 00:12:55.485
to feel that I'm obligated to do anything.

00:12:56.025 --> 00:12:57.194
Uh, pressure is bad.

00:12:57.255 --> 00:13:00.704
I mean, if you, if you want to make sure
you can't remember something, just put

00:13:00.704 --> 00:13:02.564
pressure on yourself to remember it.

00:13:02.834 --> 00:13:03.735
Pressure is bad.

00:13:04.035 --> 00:13:06.795
So it's an open-ended goal.

00:13:07.334 --> 00:13:08.595
Just I want to improve.

00:13:08.595 --> 00:13:11.685
I want to continue enjoying these
languages and I want to improve.

00:13:11.745 --> 00:13:17.145
If I discover that the Punjabi is too
difficult or Tagalog, then I will have

00:13:17.145 --> 00:13:19.515
no hesitation in abandoning those things.

00:13:19.515 --> 00:13:20.775
There's no pressure on me.

00:13:21.135 --> 00:13:25.305
Uh, I think I previously said that
I wanted to get to a level in,

00:13:25.555 --> 00:13:28.444
in Arabic where I can understand
podcasts and I'll just hear it.

00:13:28.465 --> 00:13:29.895
I'm still not there.

00:13:30.075 --> 00:13:30.615
Okay.

00:13:30.735 --> 00:13:35.444
And I got, you know, diverted by the
way, I do want to get back to Turkish.

00:13:35.685 --> 00:13:40.415
So no firm commitments
and Turkish is still out.

00:13:41.265 --> 00:13:45.435
Uh, particularly if at some point my
wife and I plan to travel to that part

00:13:45.435 --> 00:13:50.954
of the world, but more immediately, as
I say, Punjabi, explore Punjabi, export

00:13:50.954 --> 00:13:54.675
that dialogue and continue working
on improving my Arabic and Persian.

00:13:54.795 --> 00:13:55.935
Those are my language goals.

00:13:56.324 --> 00:14:01.244
The next video I do will be about some
of the different approaches I'm going to

00:14:01.244 --> 00:14:03.435
take to my language learning activities.

00:14:04.155 --> 00:14:09.190
And, uh, I'll leave you with a couple
of videos where I talk about how to get

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.000
ourselves going in language learning.

